 kit audit - lists the forge's attestations, alerts, controls, and waivers, and the policy's rules

   one of
   *kit audit*  lists the forge's attestations, alerts, controls, and waivers, and the policy's rules

     --text   --json   --jq
                           prints the stages as text, even on a terminal

   --stages   prints the stages, one per line, in order, the same over any workspace
   --marks    prints a mark per stage, as one JSON object: evidence's moves with the forge's runs
              and releases, findings' with its runs and alerts, each scanner's off or refused where
              it is, policy's with kit.yml and .kit/policy; controls and waivers have none, null, as
              each is read again only on Refresh, kittui offering no Watch for them
   evidence   {subject, digest, kind, signer, age}, keyed by the digest and the kind: subject the
              asset's or artifact's name; kind the statement's predicate type, null where GitHub
              keeps the bundle apart, compressed, which kit does not read; signer the workflow that
              signed it, <owner>/<repo>/<path>@<ref>, github for one GitHub made itself, or null;
              age in seconds. The subject links to the repository's attestations. GitHub reads a
              digest's attestations a request each, 50 digests at most; past 5 releases, 20
              artifacts, or 50 digests, the document is limited. GitLab and Azure DevOps keep none
              kit reads
   findings   {tool, number, rule, severity, state, path, age}, keyed by the tool and the number:
              tool code-scanning, dependabot, or secret-scanning; rule what found it, a rule's id,
              an advisory's, or a secret's type; severity in lower case, null for a secret; state
              open or dismissed; path where it was found. The rule links to the alert's page. GitHub
              reads each scanner's every page; a refusal names the scope to add, gh auth refresh
              --scopes security_events, or who can grant the access. GitLab reads its vulnerability
              report, SAST for code scanning, dependency scanning for Dependabot, and secret
              detection, off on a tier without it; GitLab leaves out a vulnerability the caller may
              not see, so the tab reads empty, never refused. Azure DevOps keeps none kit reads
   controls   {id, control, target, enforcement, rules, source}, keyed by the id: control its name;
              target the refs it names; enforcement active, evaluate, or disabled; rules its kinds
              of rule, null where the forge shows them only to an admin; source ruleset or
              protection. The control links to its page. GitHub reads its rulesets, its own and its
              organization's, in one GraphQL request, and the default branch's protection; GitLab
              its protected branches and push rules; Azure DevOps the default branch's policies
   waivers    {waives, actor, kind, reason, by, until, hidden}, keyed by what it waives and the
              actor: waives a control's id, or an alert as <tool> <number>, each linking that row;
              actor who may bypass; kind bypass or dismissal; reason the bypass's mode, or the
              dismissal's reason and comment; by who dismissed it; until when it ends, null for
              never; hidden, on the one bypass row of a control's bypassers the forge hides or kit
              cannot name, keyed <id> hidden, how many, its actor null, else null. The reason links
              to its page. The bypasses are the forge's own, GitHub's rulesets' bypass actors and
              its protection's; the dismissals are the alerts findings reads, each scanner read once
              for both, and a scanner off here, or one that refuses the caller, is under errors as
              in findings while the rest still draw. GitLab's are its dismissed vulnerabilities;
              Azure DevOps keeps none kit reads
   policy     {rule, pattern, from, engine}, keyed by the rule, as kit policy --rules --json prints
              them


 kit audit
